WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.120
Speaker 1: We're going to start today in&nbsp;
Australia, where progressives scored a massive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.120 --> 00:00:09.840
victory in the Australian elections. Those&nbsp;
were on Saturday, dozens of you emailing me&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.840 --> 00:00:14.240
about this and saying, David, you've got to cover&nbsp;
what's happening in Australia, because not only&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.240 --> 00:00:21.760
is it a massive victory for the left, it's also&nbsp;
a big signal about what could be possible maybe&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.400 --> 00:00:28.320
in the United States if the United States became&nbsp;
significantly less crazy. And we'll get to that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.320 --> 00:00:33.360
in a moment. The Liberal Party in Australia,&nbsp;
led by the Prime Minister Scott Morrison,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.360 --> 00:00:39.760
wanted a fourth term in a row and the opposition,&nbsp;
the main opposition, the strongest opposition was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.760 --> 00:00:47.040
the left wing Labor Party led by Anthony Albanese.&nbsp;
Votes are still being counted, but it does appear&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.040 --> 00:00:52.560
as of this morning as though the Labor Party will&nbsp;
have the ability to form a government due to some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.560 --> 00:00:57.600
major wins that they had. And as of right now and&nbsp;
again, they're still counting votes. These numbers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.600 --> 00:01:05.280
may change. As of this morning, I should say this&nbsp;
morning, Australia time. Labor looks to be up to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.280 --> 00:01:16.320
73 seats, while the Liberal Party appears to have&nbsp;
lost 24 seats from 75 before the election down to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.320 --> 00:01:26.320
only 51. This is a big defeat for the centrist&nbsp;
Liberal Party and it is a crushing victory for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.320 --> 00:01:33.440
the left wing Labor Party. Now also of note, the&nbsp;
Green Party, led by Adam Bandt, appears to have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.440 --> 00:01:41.200
secured three seats. They previously had one, so&nbsp;
gaining too from 1 to 3 seats. As of this morning,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.200 --> 00:01:47.200
the Green Party, although the final numbers may&nbsp;
change a little bit. So this would make Anthony&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.200 --> 00:01:52.880
Albanese the fourth Labor leader. And by the way,&nbsp;
I hope I'm pronouncing that correctly, the fourth&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.880 --> 00:01:59.920
Labor leader to win control of the government&nbsp;
since World War two. So the first question is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.920 --> 00:02:08.160
how on earth did Labor achieve this? What did they&nbsp;
run on? Well, a huge component of Labor's 2022&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.160 --> 00:02:18.000
platform was climate change. It was a platform of&nbsp;
renewal, not revolution and Labor making a number&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.000 --> 00:02:22.880
of promises along the lines of the environment&nbsp;
and dealing with climate change. Labor promised&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.880 --> 00:02:32.800
to nearly double Australia's 2030 target cuts to&nbsp;
carbon emissions, saying let's cut by 43% by 2030&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.800 --> 00:02:38.400
and be it completely net zero by 2050. Very&nbsp;
ambitious. Is it possible to do that? Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.400 --> 00:02:45.120
time will tell. But they ran on it and the voters&nbsp;
liked it. Transitioning to renewable energy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.120 --> 00:02:50.880
upgrade the nation's energy grid, roll out&nbsp;
solar banks and community batteries have meant,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.880 --> 00:02:55.120
as many of you know, it's great that solar&nbsp;
panels are becoming more and more efficient,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.120 --> 00:03:00.400
but you've got to be able to store that energy&nbsp;
to really increase the viability of solar&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.040 --> 00:03:07.200
at a larger level. The Labor Party said&nbsp;
that they would approve new coal mines&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.920 --> 00:03:13.440
if they stack up environmentally and economically.&nbsp;
So not saying we're not doing any more fossil&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.440 --> 00:03:19.840
fuels, but saying it's really got to make sense&nbsp;
in context. A promise to keep the Australian Great&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.840 --> 00:03:26.800
Barrier Reef more protected, to protect endangered&nbsp;
species, to do more about bushfire recovery, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.800 --> 00:03:33.120
also campaigned on increasing Australia's federal&nbsp;
national minimum wage, more funding for childcare&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.120 --> 00:03:40.560
centers, nursing homes and disability services.&nbsp;
And understand that this is not communism, this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.560 --> 00:03:46.000
is not socialism. There were right wingers all&nbsp;
over Twitter and all over Troth, Essential and all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.000 --> 00:03:51.040
of these other places saying Australia has gone&nbsp;
completely communist. Now the really funny thing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.040 --> 00:03:56.160
is the very many of the very same people would&nbsp;
say that the Liberal Party was also Communist.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.160 --> 00:04:04.320
Now understand that this is true what I would call&nbsp;
left, center left. You've got the center, you've&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.320 --> 00:04:09.200
got the center left, which would be like the&nbsp;
Liberal Party, like Justin Trudeau in Canada, more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.200 --> 00:04:13.760
or less. That's the center left. And then you've&nbsp;
got this is not far left. This is what I would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.760 --> 00:04:20.480
call left, center left. It's social democracy.&nbsp;
It's certainly to the left of Biden and Justin&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.480 --> 00:04:25.920
Trudeau. But it is not socialism and it is not&nbsp;
communism. It's basically where I am. It's social&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.920 --> 00:04:31.760
democracy in something more or less approximating&nbsp;
the Nordic model, the northern European model. So&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.560 --> 00:04:38.000
understand that many of the same people that are&nbsp;
now saying, oh, Australia has elected socialists,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.800 --> 00:04:44.960
they say Justin Trudeau is a socialist. They said&nbsp;
that the Australian Labor Party, a Liberal Party,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.960 --> 00:04:49.520
was also socialist, but they were not. So&nbsp;
these people, you can't really go to them.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.520 --> 00:04:56.800
This is going to be a major experiment.&nbsp;
Can labor in power get things done and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.800 --> 00:05:04.080
maybe even more importantly, are. The things&nbsp;
Labor wants to do actually viable will they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.080 --> 00:05:11.680
work? Because another big criticism of many of the&nbsp;
ideas that I and other Social Democrats espouse&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.680 --> 00:05:17.360
is that stuff would never work if you had the&nbsp;
opportunity to make it law, to make it policy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:17.360 --> 00:05:22.560
to actually do this stuff. It will implode your&nbsp;
country. It will be a disaster for your country.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.560 --> 00:05:28.320
Well, let's see. And depending on the level&nbsp;
of obstruction that's possible in Australia,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.960 --> 00:05:33.520
we may in a few years have some&nbsp;
answers. Do these ideas actually work?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.080 --> 00:05:40.000
Despite what the right loves to say when people&nbsp;
like me and others propose them in the United&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.000 --> 00:05:45.360
States? So it's going to be a fascinating time.&nbsp;
And this is we can't shortchange what this is.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:45.360 --> 00:05:53.200
This is a massive victory for the left. Running&nbsp;
on climate change, environmental justice,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.200 --> 00:05:58.320
economic justice, renewable energy, all of these&nbsp;
things we've been talking about. A couple of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:58.320 --> 00:06:04.880
notes. Stephanie from Australia wrote in to me and&nbsp;
said, quote, One of the most interesting aspects&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.880 --> 00:06:11.360
of this has been a large number of very wealthy,&nbsp;
traditionally Liberal Party inner city districts&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:11.360 --> 00:06:17.280
being taken by a group of loosely aligned&nbsp;
independents who are economically moderate. Right.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:17.280 --> 00:06:23.360
But pro action on climate change, political&nbsp;
integrity and gender equality. This is being seen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:23.360 --> 00:06:28.480
as a repudiation of the Conservative government's&nbsp;
move to the right towards a more Republican style.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:28.480 --> 00:06:32.640
Would an independent movement like that with&nbsp;
funding from rich, moderate conservative backers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:32.640 --> 00:06:36.720
is this the case here? Have any chance&nbsp;
in similar areas of the U.S.? Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:36.720 --> 00:06:44.480
the question in the US is. I don't know that we&nbsp;
have that group. I mean, I guess anecdotally there&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.480 --> 00:06:51.200
are some center right moderates who are quite&nbsp;
socially liberal and pro action on climate change.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.640 --> 00:06:56.160
But I don't really know that that's really a big&nbsp;
contingent. I mean, here, you know, if you if you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:56.160 --> 00:07:00.720
think of the center right in the United States&nbsp;
and I know everybody, this is fraught because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:00.720 --> 00:07:04.720
people would say those aren't really center right&nbsp;
people. If you think of the center right in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:04.720 --> 00:07:11.120
United States, which is people like Joe Manchin&nbsp;
and Mitt Romney, they don't seem big on taking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:11.120 --> 00:07:17.200
bold action on climate. They seem to really&nbsp;
want to kind of just protect business interests&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:17.200 --> 00:07:21.840
when it comes to thinking about climate. So, sure,&nbsp;
Stephanie, it would work if we had that contingent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:21.840 --> 00:07:26.880
in the United States. I don't know that we do,&nbsp;
but I'm willing to have my mind changed. Also,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:26.880 --> 00:07:32.000
our friend Josh Zips, who I've been on his show,&nbsp;
I don't remember if he's been on my show. He's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:32.000 --> 00:07:38.160
Australian. He had a good tweet, quote, The lesson&nbsp;
of the Australian election is unambiguous. If&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:38.160 --> 00:07:43.120
you refuse to address a big problem that&nbsp;
voters persistently say they care about,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:43.120 --> 00:07:48.640
they'll smack you down. Australians are fed up&nbsp;
with excuses about climate chaos. They've been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:48.640 --> 00:07:56.560
saying this for years. Welcome to democracy.&nbsp;
Hopeful results in Australia a really big deal.

